---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ChapterHead from '../components/ChapterHead.astro';
import { assetImage } from '../images';
---

<Layout chapter="sponsorships">
  <ChapterHead chapter="sponsorships" />

  <article class="prose">
    <p>
      Sponsorship activity will be governed by three core principles:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Strategic balance</strong>: the sponsorship portfolio we present here is diversified
        across academic, institutional, corporate, cultural and service partners to address the
        logistics and resources needed for the quality of the event’s realization while relying on
        financial stability. This balanced approach reduces dependency on any single funding source
        and aligns partner contributions with the event’s academic and social program.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ethical integrity</strong>: the Organizing Committee will exclude organizations
        whose primary activities are the production of weapons, active war profiteering, or
        demonstrably environmentally harmful operations. Ethical screening will be applied at the
        earliest stage of contact and embedded in sponsor agreements to protect the event’s values
        and reputation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Operational transparency</strong>: all sponsor offers, in‑kind valuations and
        contractual commitments will be recorded in a centralised pipeline. Sponsor benefits will be
        clearly described in written agreements and communicated to internal stakeholders. The
        pipeline will document contact history, proposed packages, screening outcomes and signed
        commitments to ensure accountability and auditability.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The Organizing Committee will pursue a mixed approach that combines institutional patronage,
      corporate sponsorship, public funding and targeted in‑kind partnerships.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Institutional engagement</strong> will focus on universities and research centres in
      Milano and Torino, which will be invited to contribute venue support, endorsement letters,
      invited speakers and access to laboratory visits; national scientific societies and learned
      institutions will be approached for patronage and program collaboration.
    </p>
    <p>
      Scientific patronage will be sought from the international bodies that anchor ICPS within the
      global physics community. The <strong>International Association of Physics Students (IAPS)</strong>, the
      umbrella organization under which ICPS is held each year, will lend its institutional
      endorsement and its network of national physics student societies. The{' '}
      <strong>International Union of Pure and Applied Physics (IUPAP)</strong>, the global body
      coordinating physics research and education across countries, will be approached for formal
      patronage, as is customary for major international physics gatherings. The{' '}
      <strong>European Physical Society (EPS)</strong>, representing the physics community across
      the continent, will likewise be invited to support the Conference's scientific and educational
      mission.
    </p>

    <div class="logo-row" data-od-id="logo-row-scientific-patronage">
      <span class="logo-row__label">Scientific patronage</span>
      <Image src={assetImage('sponsors/iaps.jpg')} alt="IAPS logo" />
      <Image src={assetImage('sponsors/iupap.png')} alt="IUPAP logo" />
      <Image src={assetImage('sponsors/eps.png')} alt="EPS logo" />
    </div>

    <p>
      The Committee's core institutional partners are the Departments of Physics at the{' '}
      <strong>University of Torino (UniTO)</strong> and at the <strong>University of Milano</strong> and{' '}
      <strong>Milano-Bicocca (UniMI/UniMIB)</strong>. They will be the academic anchors of the
      conference, contributing in venue support, faculty involvement, all while providing direct
      access to their research groups. The <strong>Politecnico di Torino</strong> and the{' '}
      <strong>Politecnico di Milano</strong> will be engaged as technical partners, offering
      laboratory access and excellent engineering expertise, tangibly experienced by participants in
      hands-on sessions. <strong>INRIM</strong>, Italy's national metrology institute in Torino,
      will host visits centred on its work with atomic clocks and quantum technologies used to
      define physical units of measurement. Finally, <strong>INFN</strong>'s sections in Torino and
      Milano will give participants direct access to the technologies underpinning CERN-scale
      experiments.
    </p>

    <div class="logo-row" data-od-id="logo-row-core-institutional-partners">
      <span class="logo-row__label">Core institutional partners</span>
      <Image src={assetImage('sponsors/unito.png')} alt="University of Torino logo" />
      <Image src={assetImage('sponsors/unimi.png')} alt="University of Milano logo" />
      <Image src={assetImage('sponsors/unimib.png')} alt="University of Milano-Bicocca logo" />
      <Image src={assetImage('sponsors/infn.jpg')} alt="INFN logo" />
      <Image src={assetImage('sponsors/inrim.jpg')} alt="INRiM logo" />
      <Image src={assetImage('sponsors/polimi.png')} alt="Politecnico di Milano logo" />
      <Image src={assetImage('sponsors/polito.png')} alt="Politecnico di Torino logo" />
    </div>

    <p>
      Beyond these fundamental partners, the Committee will also seek engagement from other
      respected institutions in the landscape of Italian science, including the Italian Space Agency
      (ASI), the Brera Astronomical Observatory, the Scuola Normale Superiore (SNS) and ENEA, the
      National Agency for New Technologies, Energy and Sustainable Economic Development, each of
      which could contribute an invited talk, a guided visit or a thematic session. Further
      laboratory and observatory visits, such as to the Conservation and Restoration Centre of
      Venaria Reale or the INAF Astrophysical Observatory in Pino Torinese, will also be explored on
      the same basis.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Industry engagement</strong> will foreground partnerships with the metropolitan
      industry and technology clusters of northern Italy (including technology firms, advanced
      manufacTorinog, automotive and aerospace companies, finance and life-sciences firms, and
      deep-tech startups), offering bespoke packages that combine branding, recruitment
      opportunities, sponsored workshops and direct access to early-career talent.{' '}
      <strong>Intesa Sanpaolo</strong>, one of Italy's leading banking groups, will be one of our
      main financial partners, thus supporting the conference's economic sustainability. The
      Committee will also pursue engagement with global technology players such as ABB, the
      electrification and automation leader, and Wolfram Research, the computational software
      pioneer behind Mathematica. Both are excellent partners for the organization of technical
      workshops, equipment demonstrations and hands-on computational sessions.
    </p>

    <div class="logo-row" data-od-id="logo-row-intesa-sanpaolo">
      <span class="logo-row__label">Intesa Sanpaolo</span>
      <Image src={assetImage('sponsors/intesa-sanpaolo.png')} alt="Intesa Sanpaolo logo" />
    </div>

    <p>
      <strong>Public and cultural engagement</strong> will rely on close cooperation with regional
      authorities. Municipal cultural institutions, such as the cities’ major museums, will help in
      shaping a program designed to showcase the cultural heritage of Milano and Torino. Support for
      outreach activities will be sought from public funding schemes and private foundations
      whenever appropriate.
    </p>
    <p>
      Service providers will be encouraged to supply in‑kind discounts for audiovisual production,
      catering, accommodation, transport and printing; such in‑kind contributions will be credited
      as sponsorship value and integrated into partner packages.
    </p>
    <p>
      All approaches will emphasise the <em>educational impact of ICPS</em> and the{' '}
      <em>opportunity to support the professional development of physics students</em>. Scientific
      partners will be explicitly invited to host stands and booths, propose invited talks and
      organize guided visits to laboratories and research centres, while corporate partners will
      receive measurable engagement metrics and post‑event reporting on visibility and recruitment
      outcomes.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Potential sponsors</h2>

      <h3>Institutes</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>National Institute for Nuclear Physics (INFN)</strong>: INFN anchors the
          scientific side of the conference as one of Italy's foremost authorities on experimental
          physics. It lends real academic weight to the event and can open its laboratories to
          participants while contributing keynote speakers.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>National Institute for Astrophysics (INAF)</strong>: The natural point of contact
          for anything astronomy-related, from public outreach to full observatory visits.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>National Research Council (CNR)</strong>: A multidisciplinary research body whose
          reach spans nearly every scientific field, making it a fitting patron for the event and a
          potential collaborator on workshops that cross disciplinary boundaries.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>National Institute of Metrological Research (INRiM)</strong>: Italy's technical
          authority on measurement science. A partnership translates naturally into specialist
          workshops and hands-on demonstrations in metrology and instrumentation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ministry of University and Research (MUR)</strong>: The primary national point of
          contact for the university and research sector, and therefore essential for securing
          institutional patronage, formal recognition, and access to funding programs or mobility
          grants tied to scientific activities.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ministry of Culture (MiC)</strong>: A key partner for the event's public-facing
          side, opening doors to museums and cultural venues while offering formal patronage that
          lends institutional weight to evening public programming.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Italian Space Agency (ASI)</strong>: As Italy's reference point for all things
          space, ASI carries high institutional visibility and could contribute both speakers and
          technical content to space-themed sessions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>HPC Centre / ICSC</strong>: Supplies the computational muscle behind any HPC or
          data-science workshop, offering resources and hands-on expertise to participants.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Region of Lombardy</strong>: The key regional authority in the Milano area,
          positioned to offer patronage, regional funding, and logistical backing on the ground. Its
          Office for Research and Innovation is a further point of contact for co-funding and
          university-industry initiatives at the regional level.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Region of Piedmont</strong>: A regional authority whose patronage, local
          contributions, and support for internationalisation could meaningfully strengthen the
          Torino leg of the program. Its Office for Research and Universities focuses specifically
          on university internationalisation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Municipality of Milano (Assessorate for Culture / University)</strong>: A
          municipal partner able to grant formal patronage, open access to city venues, and help
          promote the event across Milano's urban fabric.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Municipality of Torino (Assessorate for Culture / Tourism)</strong>: Torino's
          local interlocutor for patronage and event logistics, particularly well positioned to help
          integrate evening events into the city's broader cultural offer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Metropolitan City of Milano</strong>: Coordinates transport and services at the
          metropolitan scale; useful for promotion efforts that need to reach beyond the city
          centre.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Metropolitan City of Torino</strong>: The Torino equivalent, handling local
          coordination, logistics, and territorial promotion across the wider metropolitan area.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>University of Milano</strong>: A major public university with strong physics and
          interdisciplinary departments, well suited to serve as a host partner. Beyond venues, it
          could supply faculty speakers, laboratory visits, and a pool of student volunteers, all
          while lending institutional credibility to the event.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Politecnico di Milano</strong>: A leading technical university whose strength lies
          in applied physics, engineering, and industry relations: a strategic partner for
          technology-focused workshops, laboratory demonstrations, and any activity that calls for
          corporate liaison.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>University of Torino</strong>: The principal academic partner in Torino, offering
          local venues and departmental expertise.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Politecnico di Torino</strong>: Closely tied to the automotive, aerospace, and
          advanced manufacturing clusters of the region, making it a natural fit for career events,
          industry engagement, and applied-technology sessions with a hands-on component.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pino Torinese Observatory and Planetarium</strong>: A regional observatory and
          planetarium built for high-impact public outreach.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Brera Astronomical Observatory</strong>: A historic research observatory with deep
          outreach experience, ideal for guided visits, public lectures, and specialist talks tied
          to the astronomy strand of the program.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>National Centre for Oncological Hadrontherapy (CNAO)</strong>: A clinical and
          research centre specialising in hadron therapy, and a natural fit for medical-physics
          sessions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>FIRC Institute of Molecular Oncology (IFOM)</strong>: A biomedical research
          institute with strong translational and molecular expertise, ideal for interdisciplinary
          sessions that link physics with biology and medicine through imaging and instrumentation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>International School for Advanced Studies (SISSA)</strong>: An internationally
          recognised graduate school with particular strength in theoretical and computational
          physics, neuroscience, and applied mathematics.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scuola Normale Superiore (SNS)</strong>: One of Italy's most selective research
          and teaching institutions, distinguished in fundamental physics and mathematics. A
          strategic partner for attracting distinguished speakers and engaging top-level graduate
          students in advanced topical sessions.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Merate Observatory</strong>: A regional INAF observatory that offers an appealing
          alternative excursion site, well suited to astronomy outreach and technical demonstrations.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Academy of Sciences of Torino (AST)</strong>: A historic scientific academy that
          lends institutional endorsement and authoritative visibility to scientific programming,
          with expert speakers and archival venues of its own to offer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Torino Astronomical Observatory (OATo)</strong>: A regional astronomical
          observatory offering guided visits and planetarium sessions, backed by outreach staff
          experienced in high-impact public evenings.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong
            >National Agency for New Technologies, Energy and Sustainable Economic Development
            (ENEA)</strong
          >: A national research agency positioned to contribute expert speakers on energy and
          sustainability, alongside institutional support for the applied-science tracks of the
          program.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Conservation and Restoration Centre of Venaria Reale</strong>: One of Europe's
          foremost centres for the conservation of cultural heritage, where physics, chemistry,
          materials science, and advanced imaging converge in the preservation of artworks,
          archaeological artefacts, and historical monuments.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Companies &amp; industries</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>CESI</strong>: A research and testing centre in electrical engineering, able to
          bring technical workshops and real industry case studies on energy systems into the
          applied-physics tracks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>ABB</strong>: A global leader in electrification and automation, positioned to
          sponsor technical sessions and equipment demonstrations while offering strong corporate
          branding and recruitment engagement across the conference.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>SAES Getters</strong>: An advanced materials company whose sponsorship could
          support materials-science sessions and equipment demonstrations, alongside broader
          industry-academic collaboration.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Huawei</strong>: A technology partner bringing ICT expertise and demonstration
          equipment to technical sessions on 5G and advanced communications.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fondazione Bracco</strong>: A philanthropic foundation supporting science and
          education, with the capacity to fund outreach strands, travel scholarships, and
          high-visibility cultural programming.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fondazione Cariplo</strong>: A leading philanthropic foundation with a strong
          track record in underwriting outreach and travel scholarships, well-positioned to support
          the event's legacy and long-term impact.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Zanichelli</strong>: A leading Italian academic publisher offering a natural
          publishing partnership, from conference proceedings to promotional reach into academic
          audiences and support for student-facing educational resources.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Wolfram Research</strong>: A global leader in computational software and knowledge
          engines, able to provide software licences and workshop content while giving students
          hands-on access to Wolfram technologies for data-driven sessions.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Collaborations</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Confindustria</strong>: The national employers' federation, offering high-level
          institutional endorsement and a direct line into industry networks and corporate delegates
          for panels, round-tables, and recruitment outreach.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Manifestazioni UniMI</strong>: A campus events partner able to supply prime
          lecture halls and logistical coordination, backed by on-site staff and university-wide
          promotion to maximise student turnout.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>CNA</strong>: A national SME confederation with strong local networks, useful for
          mobilising regional small-business partners and sourcing vetted technical suppliers, as
          well as facilitating in-kind services from local enterprises.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Centro Scienza</strong>: An interactive science centre bringing hands-on exhibits
          and outreach workshops, with experience running school engagement and family-oriented
          public events that could integrate directly into the conference schedule.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>INFINITO Science Centre</strong>: An immersive science museum and outreach hub,
          offering exhibition space and interactive demonstrations that could broaden the event's
          public reach.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>CICAP</strong>: A science-communication organization skilled in critical-thinking
          outreach, well suited to public debates and media-facing events that strengthen the
          scientific-literacy strand of the program.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
